    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. Can you go a bit deeper into the contingency planning part? Specifically, what should the protocol look like if a shipment gets delayed at a customs checkpoint or a remote transit hub where there isn't immediate access to specialized refrigeration? We're worried about \"interlopers\" or unexpected disruptions in the chain that might compromise the integrity of the cargo.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If delays occur at customs or remote hubs, the protocol must first immediately stabilize the temperature using backup power or supplementary cooling agents to buy time. Next, you need pre-vetted contingency agreements with local third-party logistics (3PL) providers who are certified to maintain the required cold chain standards in that specific region. Finally, a rapid risk assessment should be conducted by the shipper's QC team to determine if the thermal profile degradation necessitates immediate quarantine or rejection of the shipment.",
      "source": "target",
